UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 9, Lesson 1: Multiplication facts to 10 x 10', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Multiplication facts to 10 × 10 are products of two factors; arrays and turnarounds (commutativity) help you learn related facts.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nGive a turnaround and an array description for 6 × 9.\n\nFEEDBACK\n9 × 6 = 54; array six rows of nine (or nine of six).\n\nWATCH FOR\nCounting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nComplete a full 0–10 × 0–10 practice grid (or forty mixed facts). Highlight five turnaround pairs and five facts you will practise next.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Multiplication facts to 10 × 10 are products of two factors; arrays and turnarounds (commutativity) help you learn related facts.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Concept","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-MR-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Complete a full 0–10 × 0–10 practice grid (or forty mixed facts). Highlight five turnaround pairs and five facts you will practise next. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for times tables; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Counting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals.. Correction uses 9 × 6 = 54; array six rows of nine (or nine of six).. Why: restores accurate times tables.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Counting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses times tables with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with times tables, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for times tables, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers. / Accept a named misconception about times tables, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for times tables. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Counting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","9 × 6 = 54; array six rows of nine (or nine of six)"],"question":"Give a turnaround and an array description for 6 × 9. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for times tables; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of factor and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Counting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals","Multiplication facts to 10 × 10 are products of two factors","9 × 6 = 54; array six rows of nine (or nine of six)"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with times tables?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named factor and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Counting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals","Name factor and explain why it matters only, without linking it to times tables","Apply times tables to new material and name the factor and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies times tables with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value, quantity or the asked operation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Counting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals","Show each place, value or step and say why that step is valid","Write only a final number with no working","Change digits to look neater without checking place value"],"question":"Which working best supports a correct answer for this lesson on times tables?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the taught times tables method; the others break place value, units or the asked quantity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore units and treat 6 and 9 as interchangeable","9 × 6 = 54; array six rows of nine (or nine of six)","Counting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals","Read 6 from left to right as ones only, ignoring place value"],"question":"Using the method for times tables, which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus, not by blending answers. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Delete units so the answers appear to match","Average the two answers without checking either","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed","Pick the longer-looking working automatically"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a times tables problem with numbers such as 6 and 9, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use the same times tables method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter","Retype the worked model numbers 6 and 9 as the answer","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers","Counting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals"],"question":"Solve a fresh times tables problem that uses different numbers from the worked model and explain one key step.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Counting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals","Multiplication facts to 10 × 10 are products of two factors","9 × 6 = 54; array six rows of nine (or nine of six)","Label units when comparing 6 with 9"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for times tables?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for times tables, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses times tables with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for times tables; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about times tables, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with times tables, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Counting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals.. Correction uses 9 × 6 = 54; array six rows of nine (or nine of six).. Why: restores accurate times tables.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y4-w09-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise times tables.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number and Algebra","success_criteria":["I can use times tables with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify factor and explain why it matters.","I can practise product independently on a new prompt.","I can notice array needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Multiplication facts to 10 × 10 are products of two factors; arrays and turnarounds (commutativity) help you learn related facts.","options":["Multiplication facts to 10 × 10 are products of two factors","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about times tables with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n7 × 8 = 56. Array: 7 rows of 8. Turnaround: 8 × 7 = 56. Near fact: 7 × 10 − 7 × 2 = 70 − 14 = 56.\n\nTRY FIRST\nGive a turnaround and an array description for 6 × 9.\n\nFEEDBACK\n9 × 6 = 54; array six rows of nine (or nine of six).","explain":"Multiplication facts to 10 × 10 are products of two factors; arrays and turnarounds (commutativity) help you learn related facts.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Counting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Fact with array and commutativity.","caption":"Near facts support memory.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Array</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">7×8 rows</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Turnaround</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">8×7=56</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: 7 × 8 = 56. Array: 7 rows of 8. Turnaround: 8 × 7 = 56. Near fact: 7 × 10 − 7 × 2 = 70 − 14 = 56. Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: 7 × 8 = 56. Array: 7 rows of 8. Turnaround: 8 × 7 = 56. Near fact: 7 × 10 − 7 × 2 = 70 − 14 = 56. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about times tables with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n7 × 8 = 56. Array: 7 rows of 8. Turnaround: 8 × 7 = 56. Near fact: 7 × 10 − 7 × 2 = 70 − 14 = 56. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Fact fluency contrasted with ones-counting.","caption":"Highlight turnaround pairs.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Product</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">56</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">count by ones only</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about times tables with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCounting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals. Here is a more productive model: 7 × 8 = 56. Array: 7 rows of 8. Turnaround: 8 × 7 = 56. Near fact: 7 × 10 − 7 × 2 = 70 − 14 = 56.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\n9 × 6 = 54; array six rows of nine (or nine of six).","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Counting by ones instead of using known facts is inefficient for fluency goals. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about times tables with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nMultiplication facts to 10 × 10 are products of two factors; arrays and turnarounds (commutativity) help you learn related facts. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 9, Lesson 1: Multiplication facts to 10 x 10","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Multiplication and division","week":9,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\n7 × 8 = 56. Array: 7 rows of 8. Turnaround: 8 × 7 = 56. Near fact: 7 × 10 − 7 × 2 = 70 − 14 = 56.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Fact with array and commutativity.","caption":"Near facts support memory.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Array</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">7×8 rows</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Turnaround</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">8×7=56</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Fact fluency contrasted with ones-counting.","caption":"Highlight turnaround pairs.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Product</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">56</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">count by ones only</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 4"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-mat-y4-w09-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
